[2026-03-14T22:24:11.143Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01T8bzx5EJkHvKmqxzf6Mm2i","type":"tool_result","content":"# Replay CLI Skill Overview\n\nThe Replay CLI is a tool for recording application sessions to gather performance data and debug issues. Here's what you need to know:\n\n## Quick Start\n\nInstall globally via npm, yarn, pnpm, or bun:\n```sh\nnpm i -g replayio\n```\n\nSet up authentication by creating an API key at https://app.replay.io settings and storing it in the `REPLAY_API_KEY` environment variable.\n\n## Key Commands\n\nThe main operations include:\n\n- **`record [url]`** - Launch Replay Browser to capture a session (defaults to `about:blank`)\n- **`upload [ids...]`** - Push recordings to the cloud\n- **`list`** - View all local recordings with metadata\n- **`remove [ids...]`** - Delete recordings locally\n- **`login/logout`** - Manage account access\n- **`info`** - Check installed Replay dependencies\n- **`update`** - Update the Replay Browser component\n\n## Core Workflow\n\nRecord a session at a specific URL, then upload it for debugging. If you skip the automatic upload prompt after recording, manually upload using the recording ID.\n\n## Advanced Features\n\nThe `upload-source-maps` command allows mapping source files to recordings using options like `--group`, `--extensions`, and `--ignore` for workspace management.\n\nYou can also integrate Replay's MCP Server with Claude for enhanced debugging capabilities via the specified configuration."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7e70901b-6c05-40ea-8ec0-6bdca5f0ceb4","uuid":"37478675-c369-45b7-a2fe-003f9450dabf","tool_use_result":{"bytes":4854,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Replay CLI Skill Overview\n\nThe Replay CLI is a tool for recording application sessions to gather performance data and debug issues. Here's what you need to know:\n\n## Quick Start\n\nInstall globally via npm, yarn, pnpm, or bun:\n```sh\nnpm i -g replayio\n```\n\nSet up authentication by creating an API key at https://app.replay.io settings and storing it in the `REPLAY_API_KEY` environment variable.\n\n## Key Commands\n\nThe main operations include:\n\n- **`record [url]`** - Launch Replay Browser to capture a session (defaults to `about:blank`)\n- **`upload [ids...]`** - Push recordings to the cloud\n- **`list`** - View all local recordings with metadata\n- **`remove [ids...]`** - Delete recordings locally\n- **`login/logout`** - Manage account access\n- **`info`** - Check installed Replay dependencies\n- **`update`** - Update the Replay Browser component\n\n## Core Workflow\n\nRecord a session at a specific URL, then upload it for debugging. If you skip the automatic upload prompt after recording, manually upload using the recording ID.\n\n## Advanced Features\n\nThe `upload-source-maps` command allows mapping source files to recordings using options like `--group`, `--extensions`, and `--ignore` for workspace management.\n\nYou can also integrate Replay's MCP Server with Claude for enhanced debugging capabilities via the specified configuration.","durationMs":3612,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/replayio/skills/refs/heads/main/skills/replay-cli/SKILL.md"}}
[2026-03-14T22:24:13.306Z] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019WzpwMRz11Qw4BSRbhkkTn","type":"tool_result","content":"# Replay MCP Skill File Content\n\n**Name:** replay-mcp\n\n**Description:** \"Use Replay MCP to inspect the contents of https://replay.io recordings.\"\n\n**Allowed Tools:** Bash(replayio:*), mcp__replay\n\n## Key Capabilities\n\nReplay MCP enables investigation of browser recordings by treating them as comprehensive databases of application execution. The system captures every action and event, allowing analysts to explore behavior systematically.\n\n## Investigation Framework\n\nThe documentation recommends a structured four-step approach:\n1. Frame investigative questions clearly\n2. Use exploratory tools to identify relevant events\n3. Apply explanatory tools to understand causation\n4. Form evidence-based conclusions\n\n## Tool Categories\n\n**Exploratory Tools** discover what happened and when:\n- Error detection (uncaught exceptions, React errors)\n- Timeline analysis (console messages, network requests, storage access, React renders)\n- Source code inspection (find files, read contents, search patterns)\n\n**Explanatory Tools** clarify why events occurred:\n- Dependency tracking to understand cause-and-effect relationships\n- Point descriptions with variable values\n- DOM element inspection and React component analysis\n- Expression evaluation and value logging\n\n## Practical Recommendations\n\nThe guide emphasizes locating relevant code, verifying execution, and using the Logpoint tool to monitor expression values across execution points. For timing issues, the ControlDependency tool helps identify prerequisite events and delays."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7e70901b-6c05-40ea-8ec0-6bdca5f0ceb4","uuid":"56fe74a4-82fc-4be5-99a2-2cd841a470d4","tool_use_result":{"bytes":4555,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Replay MCP Skill File Content\n\n**Name:** replay-mcp\n\n**Description:** \"U